{
 "id": "l14",
 "label": "14",
 "title": "-Um- and mag- verb forms",
 "summary": "Doer-focused verbs: making the -um- and mag- forms in every tense, and the two sentence patterns",
 "minutes": 40,
 "goals": [
  "Know what -um- and mag- verbs do: they put the focus on the doer",
  "Tell an infix (-um-) from a prefix (mag-)",
  "Make the infinitive, imperative, past, present and future of -um- and mag- verbs",
  "Learn a set of common -um- and mag- verbs",
  "Use the two sentence patterns, verb first"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "Verbs that focus on the doer",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Sentences with **-um-** and **mag-** verbs are much like the English **active voice**: the subject is the **doer** of the action. (Many English active sentences, though, would be put into the equivalent of the passive voice by Tagalog speakers.)"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Tagalog speakers like the **inverted word order**, where the **predicate with the verb comes first**, because to them that is the most important part of the sentence."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "The most common verbs in these subject-focused sentences have an **-um- infix** or a **mag- prefix**. These verbs stress the **doer** of the action, or the **act itself**. They can be used with no object at all, or with an object when the emphasis is on the doer."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Term",
      "What it is",
      "Example"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "**Infix**",
       "a piece put **inside** a root word, to make a new word",
       "k**um**ain (kain + -um-)"
      ],
      [
       "**Prefix**",
       "a piece attached to the **front** of a root word",
       "**mag**lutò (mag- + lutò)"
      ],
      [
       "**Root**",
       "the basic word that the affixes are added to",
       "kain, lutò"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Words about words"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "english",
     "title": "Derivatives",
     "t": "A **derivative** is a new word formed from a root plus an affix, such as umalís from alís."
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "-Um-, mag-, or both"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Some verb roots take **only -um-** (for example **alís**, which the book here glosses *to go*). Others take **only mag-** (for example **labá**, *to wash*). Some take **both** (such as **hingî**, *to ask for*), but the two derivatives then have **different meanings**:"
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "",
      "-Um- form",
      "Mag- form"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Kind of action",
       "non-intensive or casual: **kumakáin** (is/are eating)",
       "frequent and intense: **nagkákakáin** (to always be eating, to continually be eating)"
      ],
      [
       "Direction of action",
       "toward the doer: **bumibilí** (is/are buying)",
       "away from the doer: **magbibilí** (is/are selling)"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "When a root takes both"
    }
   ]
  },
